# numpy

## Introduction

A NumPy matrix-multiply benchmark. It times `np.matmul` on two large
random `n × n` matrices, exercising the multithreaded BLAS backend
(OpenBLAS/MKL) that NumPy links against. It is a quick check of
multi-core dense linear-algebra throughput on a node.

## Quick Start

```bash
cd /orcd/data/orcd/022/benchmarks/numpy
srun -p mit_normal -n 16 -t 15 --mem=20G bash -c \
  'module load miniforge/23.11.0-0; for t in 1 16; do OMP_NUM_THREADS=$t python mat_mult.py; done'
# prints "<s> seconds to matrix multiply"; GFLOP/s = 16000 / s for n = 20000
```

## Installation

See [install.md](install.md). The `miniforge/23.11.0-0` module already has
NumPy + OpenBLAS. Fix `output_dir` in the `job_*_all_example.sh` scripts before
the first run.

## Usage

### Automated, many runs — `job_*_all_example.sh`

`job_full_all_example.sh` and `job_half_all_example.sh` submit one job per node
in `nodes=(...)`. Each job runs `mat_mult.py` once per `OMP_NUM_THREADS` value:
1, 2, 4, … then cores/2, cores, 1.5 × cores and 2 × cores. "full" uses all
`cpu_count` cores and "half" uses half. Edit `nodes`, `partition`, `cpu_count`
and `output_dir` at the top, then run from this dir (the job calls
`python mat_mult.py` with a relative path):

```bash
cd /orcd/data/orcd/022/benchmarks/numpy
./job_full_all_example.sh      # -> <output_dir>/out_full.<node>-<jobid>
```

### Single run — root dir

```bash
module load miniforge/23.11.0-0
OMP_NUM_THREADS=8 python mat_mult.py      # n = 20000 is set inside the script
```

The script times only the `np.matmul` call, not the creation of the random
arrays. `OMP_NUM_THREADS` sets the OpenBLAS thread count.

## Analysis

Each run prints `<seconds> seconds to matrix multiply`. In the sweep output,
each result follows its `Ran with OMP_NUM_THREADS=<n>` line:

```bash
grep -E 'OMP_NUM_THREADS|seconds' <output_dir>/out_full.<node>-<jobid> | paste - -
```

The work is 2·n³ FLOPs, so for n = 20000, GFLOP/s = 16000 / seconds. Lower time
is better. Time should fall roughly linearly up to the physical core count and
flatten beyond it (hyper-threads add little for DGEMM). Compare nodes of the
same CPU type at the same thread count. A node clearly slower than its peers
points to clock, thermal or memory-config problems.
